==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=welfare}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|welfare|lang=enm}}, probably from the {{etyl|ang|en}} phrase {{term|wel faran||to fare well, get along successfully, prosper|lang=ang}}  (cognate with {{etyl|gml|-}} {{term|wolvare||welfare|lang=gml}}, Old Norse {{term|velferð|lang=non}}, Swedish {{term|välfärd|lang=sv}}, German {{term|Wohlfahrt|lang=de}} and Dutch {{term|welvaart|lang=nl}}.) Equivalent to {{compound|well|fare|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈwɛlˌfɛə/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˈwɛlˌfɛɚ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{context|uncountable|lang=en}} [[health|Health]], [[safety]], [[happiness]] and [[prosperity]]; [[well-being]] in any respect.
#* {{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=19
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=Nothing was too small to receive attention, if a supervising eye could suggest improvements likely to conduce to the common '''welfare'''. Mr. Gordon Burnage, for instance, personally visited dust-bins and back premises, accompanied by a sort of village bailiff, going his round like a commanding officer doing billets.}}
# {{context|uncountable|chiefly|US|lang=en}} Various forms of [[financial]] aid provided by the [[government]] to those who are in need of it (abbreviated form of ''Welfare assistance'').

